{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 120,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 3180,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space is big. Really big. You just won't \nbelieve how vastly hugely mind-bogglingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8940,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big it is. In fact, the near 100 billion \nlight year wide observable universe is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13740,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only a tiny fraction of the greater bigness \nof space beyond. And for a little while,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20160,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we will see more and more of that universe beyond until the cosmological horizons collapse on us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26160,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and cut off our view forever. So how long do we \nhave, and what will we see before that happens?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37560,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is an absolute limit to our access to the universe beyond our own galaxy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41700,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a limit to what we can ever \nhope to explore or send signals to,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46080,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a very different limit to \nwhat we can ever hope to witness.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50220,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to explore the latter. We’re \ngoing to figure out the absolute limit of our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55380,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "future view of the universe, and of the universe’s \nability to influence us. Next time we’ll turn it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62100,
    "dDurationMs": 6735,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around and ask: how much of the external universe \ncan we potentially influence, and even explore?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68835,
    "dDurationMs": 6225,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But let’s start by thinking about the light that we can see from the distant universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75060,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know that light doesn’t travel instantaneously. \nThat means when we look into the distance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79980,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’re seeing objects as they were in the \npast. We see the moon as it was 1.3 seconds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87120,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ago, the Sun as it was 8 minutes ago, and Milky \nWay stars as they were 100s to many 1000s of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92400,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years ago. With our greatest telescopes like \nthe James Webb we’ve seen galaxies whose light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97860,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has been travelling to us for many billions of \nyears. This is old light from a younger universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104760,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although the night sky looks like \na sphere surrounding the Earth,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can imagine it as a set of shells, which \neach shell representing light that has come to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115920,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us from a particular distance.The most distant \nlight we can see is from the cosmic microwave  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122220,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "background radiation - the CMB - which has \nbeen travelling to us for almost the entire  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127140,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "age of the universe—13.7 billion years more \nor less. This is the light emitted by the hot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133620,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hydrogen plasma around 300,000 years after \nthe big bang when the universe became cool  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 138480,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to form the first atoms, and at \nthe same time first became transparent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143460,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surely this is the limit of our \nvision, so how far away is the CMB?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, this is where things get confusing. When \nthe CMB light started its journey towards us,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155400,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe was 1100 times smaller than it is \ntoday. A photon of CMB light observed today was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162660,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emitted from particles of primordial plasma that \nwere around 40 million light years away from the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168840,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "region that would one day become the Milky \nWay. That’s right next door on cosmological  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173340,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scales. But that light was running the wrong \nway against the cosmic travelator. The expanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179820,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "universe forced it to travel 13.7 billion \nlight years in order to reach us just now.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 186360,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Meanwhile, the galaxies that those CMB blobs \nwould eventually form into have now been shoved  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193020,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the opposite direction to a distance of \n46.5 billion light years by that expansion.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200280,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We often say that the observable universe is 46.5 \nbillion light years in radius because if you froze  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207180,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the expansion now, that would be the current \ndistance of the furthest objects we can see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212100,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The CMB is the practical limit of our view \nbecause at earlier times and greater distances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218220,
    "dDurationMs": 8460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe was opaque. But it’s possible that one day we’ll get signals from those times. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226680,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutrinos can pass through the dense primordial \nplasma and could take us closer to the big bang.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233220,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are events in the first \nfraction of a second that may  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236880,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have created gravitational waves whose \ninfluence might one day be detectable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241140,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The earliest time we can ever in principle \nobserve is the instant of the big bang itself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247560,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The big bang happened everywhere, but if we could \nobserve the big bang, we would be observing it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253860,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it happened on a spherical shell just \nbeyond the cosmic microwave background."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259260,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call that shell the particle horizon. It’s \nthe absolute limit of the observable universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265680,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At least for now. If we wait, signals from more \ndistant parts will have had time to reach us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272100,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The particle horizon expands, and the shell \nfrom which we observe the CMB also expands,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277620,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "changing over time. But there’s a limit to \nthe amount of universe we’ll be able to see,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283080,
    "dDurationMs": 8820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even if we wait infinite time. To figure out that \nlimit we’re going to need a spacetime diagram.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291900,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long-time watchers have seen these \nbefore, but today we’re going to do  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296760,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things with the spacetime diagram may \nnot have seen—at least not from me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301380,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Consider the spherical shells representing light \ncoming to us from earlier epochs. Now get rid of 2  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of our 3 dimensions of space so we just see a pair \nof points on each sphere in opposite directions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314520,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with arrows for the light travelling to us. Now \nsort vertically according to when that light was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320820,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emitted. This is a spacetime diagram—one dimension \nof space plotted against time. Light is travelling  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327900,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through space and time towards us, forming a light cone—in particular, our past light cone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335880,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The boundaries of the cone are at 45 \ndegrees. This is the angle light makes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340860,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on our spacetime diagram. Slower speeds \nhave steeper angles, and that means only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346620,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "things inside or on this cone can potentially \nsend a signal to or travel to our location in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354300,
    "dDurationMs": 7380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spacetime. Events outside the past lightcone \ncan’t get signals to us here in our present."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 361680,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our particle horizon is at the bottom of the light \ncone, and the CMB is just above it. But there’s a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368820,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem here. Remember we constructed this light \ncone by layering a series of shells that represent  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376020,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the current size of those regions. But the \nuniverse has been expanding, so at earlier times  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381960,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "those regions were smaller. These lines represent \nthe past and future expansion of the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389160,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The big bang singularity has all space \ncompacted into a point, after which it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395100,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expands. That expansion at first slows due to the \ngravitational pull of all matter in the universe,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402540,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but after a while dark energy takes over and the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406260,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion begins to accelerate. And that started just a few billion years ago."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410820,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the lightcone encompasses more and more \nstuff into the past, at some point it starts  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417120,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to encompass less and less physical space. It \ncomes to a point at the particle horizon. Even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423720,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the big bang, the particle horizon doesn’t \nencompass nothing, nor does it encompass  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429780,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everything. It represents a finite portion of the \ncompacted everything at the beginning of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436200,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s easier to see that if we make \na modification to our diagram.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441059,
    "dDurationMs": 5581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine we redefine the tick marks on the x-axis \nso that instead of representing absolute distance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446640,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they represent points fixed to the expanding \nspace. These could be galaxies for example.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453660,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Currently those reference galaxies follow these \nlines. But we can stretch the graph differently  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460200,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at each layer to straighten the lines, so that now \neach tick represents more and more space going up.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468480,
    "dDurationMs": 8220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll label that axis with the distances \nthose galaxies will be from us at the present moment.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476700,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But at the bottom of the diagram, each tick \nreally represents a tiny physical distance.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482640,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That distance approaches zero towards the very bottom of the plot, but at any non-zero time after the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big bang, we can see that our particle horizon \nencompasses a finite amount of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496320,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We just created a new coordinate system. These \nare comoving coordinates—they co-move with the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503340,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "expansion of the universe. Our past lightcone in \nthis system is curved—light travels many ticks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509460,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "per unit time at the bottom but fewer going up. \nLet’s make one more change—we’re going to adjust  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517020,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the time axis so that each tick represents less \nand less time as we get closer to the big bang,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522780,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the same degree that space is compressed. \nDo this right and our lightcone and all light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531060,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed paths make straight 45 degree \nlines like they’re supposed to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535440,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With our new tool in hand, we can really see how \nour particle horizon really changes over time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541560,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we move forward in time \nour past lightcone follows us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545640,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the regular coordinate system, it still \nconverges to a point, but in comoving coordinates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550620,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we see that it encompasses more and more of the \nuniverse. Naively, it might seem that there’s no  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557100,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "limit—if we want to see any distant point we \njust have to wait long enough. And that would  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563760,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be the case if it weren’t for dark energy. The fact that the expansion of our universe is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569220,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating means there’s an absolute upper limit \non how much of the universe we can ultimately see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575820,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But to understand the future limits of \nthat view, we need to understand some  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580320,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new horizons—horizons that aren’t getting \nbigger. In fact they’re racing towards us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586920,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In an expanding universe like ours, more \ndistant points seem to be moving away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593160,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from us more quickly. A point that’s far \nenough away will be moving away from us at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 598860,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the speed of light. While objects can’t \nmove through space faster than light,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603660,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space itself can move at any speed relative to other space, and it can carries objects with it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610740,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, there’s a sphere that surrounds us where the \nrecession velocity equals the speed of light. It’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617760,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "called the Hubble horizon, after Edwin Hubble, the \nguy who discovered the expansion of the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623760,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hubble horizon is currently 14 and a half billion light years away.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628920,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you might think it would be impossible to see things further than this. After all, light  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634320,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emitted by galaxies in that distant space will be pulled away from us like fish swimming against a current.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642060,
    "dDurationMs": 8595,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can see the cosmic microwave background, and that’s 46.5 billion light years. So what gives?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650655,
    "dDurationMs": 7125,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact we routinely observe galaxies that were \nmoving away from us faster than light at the time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657780,
    "dDurationMs": 7980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they emitted the light that we see. To understand \nhow this can work, imagine you’re a photon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665760,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re spat out by a bright star in a galaxy \nmillions of light years from of the Milky  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670440,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Way. Initially you find yourself in a region \nspace that’s moving away from the Milky Way at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676620,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "3c — three times your own speed of light. \nYou’re dragged with that space and so the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683100,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way seems to be racing away from you. But \nbecause you’re moving towards the Milky Way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687600,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you escape that patch of space that’s traveling \nat 3c. That patch moves in the opposite direction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693840,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to you. Now you find yourself in a region \nof space that’s traveling a little less  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698820,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quickly away from your destination. You’re still \ndragged away from the Milky Way, but not as fast.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705780,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After billions of years of losing ground, you \neventually find yourself at the Milky Way’s  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712560,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hubble horizon where space is receding at exactly \n1c - your own speed. The Milky Way finally stops  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719820,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving away from you, and as you struggle across \nthat horizon and can finally make progress.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726300,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have to retrace a lot of ground—billions of \nlight years in fact—but eventually you reach the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732540,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Milky Way, then the Earth, then fall into a \ntelescope where you reveal secrets about your  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737160,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ancient origin to a group of bipedal apes that \nevolved long, long after you started your journey."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742380,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason that light from beyond the Hubble \nhorizon can reach us is that for the first several  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 748200,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "billion years after the big bang, the Hubble \nhorizon was expanding. Galaxies were moving away  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754920,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a more or less constant speed, in fact actually \nslowing down a bit. That means the point where  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762120,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the recession speed equaled the speed of light \nwas getting further away from us. This enabled  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767760,
    "dDurationMs": 6018,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light from more distant objects to creep inside \nthe Hubble horizon and make their way to us."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773778,
    "dDurationMs": 5682,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By the way this is great news by the way—otherwise we would never have seen the CMB and probably couldn’t have  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779460,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proved that the Big Bang happened. But this won’t always be the case. Like I said, the expansion  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 785220,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the universe is accelerating. At some point \nthe Hubble horizon will stop moving away and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791340,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually collapse back on us. The boundary of \nlight-speed expansion will eventually surround  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797880,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the local group of galaxies and stop there, \nwalling us off from the rest of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803460,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only the light that can cross the Hubble horizon before it collapses can ever reach us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 809940,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So now we introduce our final horizon—the horizon \nthat’s going to determine our ultimate view of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815880,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe. This is the cosmological event horizon. It's the boundary beyond which no signal can ever be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824040,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "received, no matter how long we wait. Light coming \nfrom beyond the cosmological event horizon won’t  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830700,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "make it across the Hubble horizon before it \ncollapses. The event horizon started out much,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "much larger than the Hubble horizon—63 billion light years in comoving distance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841680,
    "dDurationMs": 7950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it shrinks over time, because light released \nat later times has less time to get to us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849630,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s now around 16 billion light years in \nradius. At some point—in 10 billion years  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855960,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "give or take—the cosmological event horizon \nwill merge with the collapsing Hubble horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863580,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At that point we’ll never be able to \nsee new events from beyond this horizon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869700,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t mean the universe will suddenly \ngo dark. This is also when we get our broadest  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 875820,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "view of the universe. Light from the most \ndistant points that we will ever see will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881760,
    "dDurationMs": 7260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "then cross the shrinking Hubble horizon just in the nick of time. Those photons will struggle against space  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889020,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that’s moving just less than light speed, and \nwill eventually make their way into our local  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894840,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "bubble of non-expanding space. They’ll carry \nwith them light from the cosmological event  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900180,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizon as it was in the beginning of time, \nwhen it was 63 billion light years in radius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906900,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that will become the final \nsize of our particle horizon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911340,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll be able to see things that are currently 63 \nbillion light years away. That’s about half again  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917580,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the size of our current view. But we won’t \nhave that view forever. While the photons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923040,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continue to trickle in from the collapsed horizon for eternity, they will be increasingly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929460,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "redshifted—sapped of energy—from their long \nfight against the outward flow of space.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 935460,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we first get close to our our full \npanorama of the universe in about 10 billion years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941460,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the galaxies will shine mostly in infrared light. After a few hundred billion years,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 948120,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even the most energetic photons will be stretched \nto the point that we’d need a radio antenna  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 954060,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "larger than our contracted Hubble horizon \nto see them. The sky would finally be dark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 960720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that is the extent of humanity’s \npotential to see the universe.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 966240,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is one way we can see further—and that’s \nby traveling as fast as possible to escape  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 972600,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our collapsing Hubble horizon. We’ll then be in a different Hubble horizon with different view.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 978780,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we’ll save that for next episode, when we \nexplore the limits of our ability to influence  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 984960,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and explore the universe. How far can humanity eventually expand and when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991920,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to leave to avoid getting trapped \nforever in our local bubble of space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 998400,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Brilliant for supporting \nPBS. Brilliant is an online learning  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002480,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "platform for STEM with hands-on, interactive \nlessons. Brilliant is for curious learners,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1007520,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "both young and old, professional and \ninexperienced. Brilliant courses teach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012200,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you how to think and solve problems \nwith interactive lessons in STEM."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1015920,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum Mechanics is foundational for \nunderstanding modern physics. And in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1020120,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Brilliant’s Quantum Mechanics course you’ll begin \nby diving deeply into the double slit experiment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1025640,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and understanding quantum states through \npolarization. But rather than just focusing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030860,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on hairy mathematics, you’ll see it work with \nhands-on experimentation. Building knowledge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036800,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that will help you understand superpositions, \nquantum entanglement and even Bell’s Theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042680,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You'll develop intuition about how Quantum \nMechanics works and the kinds of physics  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1047600,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problems it helps to solve. To learn more \nabout Brilliant, go to brilliant.org/spacetime"
    } ]
  } ]
}
